atement_sha256":"sha256:a5e37e3203800363495306a25f7cd52a3a91d46e779be9bad72257ce5b1a18f0","file":"preprints/Polynomial-removal-fails-for-ordered-binary-matrices-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","line":33,"producer_hash_method":"atlas-whitespace-normalized-text","producer_statement_sha256":"779170032e8cc4f85c77ce4830a5fbc2c15fc6de25fa1775d27d90718443eee8","provenance":{"kind":"computed","pin":"fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb","source":"preprints/Polynomial-removal-fails-for-ordered-binary-matrices-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex"},"sha256":"sha256:a5e37e3203800363495306a25f7cd52a3a91d46e779be9bad72257ce5b1a18f0","url":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/Polynomial-removal-fails-for-ordered-binary-matrices-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex#L33"},"statement_kind":"theorem","text":"\\label{thm:main}\nThere is a fixed binary $66\\times66$ matrix $H$ with the following\nproperty. For every integer $h\\ge1$, put\n\\[\n n_h=(386h+2)2^h,\\qquad \\epsilon_h=(386h+2)^{-2}.\n\\]\nThere is a binary $n_h\\times n_h$ matrix $A_h$ such that\n\\[\n \\operatorname{dist}_H(A_h)\\ge\\epsilon_h,\n \\qquad\n \\frac{N_H(A_h)}{n_h^{132}}\\le\\epsilon_h2^{-h}.\n\\]\nConsequently, no positive constants $c,C$ give a polynomial removal\nbound for this fixed $H$."}
